# WebLogic-Live-Troubleshoot-Issues.


LIVE TROUBLESHOOTING — CASE 1
Client reports:
Application is very slow. Some pages are taking 30–60 seconds to load.

1) Check OHS
         ps -ef |grep httpd
        ss -lntp |grep 9443 (check listening port)
        Test locally:- curl -klv https://$OHS_IP:9443
Check OHS Error/acess log:
       tail -f $ORACLE_INSTANCE/diagnostics/logs/OHS/ohs1/ohs1.log

   if u see "mod_wl_ohs: weblogic connection timeout" 
          it is clue,not root cause . It tell OHS is waiting for WebLogic.


2) Test OHS--->WebLogic
    from OHS:
           nc -zv 10.10.10.21 8001
           nc -zv 10.10.10.22 8001

Both reuturns connection succedded. So then OHS-->WebLogic TCP Connectivity Working fine.

Now Test Directly:

curl -v http://10.10.10.21:8001/myapp/
curl -v http://10.10.10.22:8001/myapp/

If both takes 35-50 seconds. then it is good (so probably issue is not between OHS-->WebLogic)

Step 3 — Check WebLogic runtime
Environment → Servers→ MS1 → Monitoring

Look for:
           Threads
           JVM Performance
         Data Sources / Resource Performance

Check Hogging Threads, Stuck Threads, Standby Threads and Throughput for the WebLogic thread pool.
 Hogging Threads :- A hogging thread is a thread currently held by a request; WebLogic can later classify it as stuck depending on how long it remains busy.
Stuck Threads :- if too many then, need to take thread Dump.
Idle Threads :- it is fresh thread waiting for request.

Step 4 — Take thread dumps
            jstack -l 24581 > /tmp/MS1_td_01.txt
            sleep 10
            jstack -l 24581 > /tmp/MS1_td_02.txt
            sleep 10
            jstack -l 24581 > /tmp/MS1_td_03.txt    


If the same threads have the same stack trace across all three dumps, that's a very strong indication they're consistently spending time in that operation.

